+/* -------------------------------------------------------------- five-way nav
+ *
+ * Keyboard navigation of forms, the way a Palm OS 5 device does it with its
+ * 5-way navigator: a focus ring steps through the form's objects and "select"
+ * acts on the one it is on. The keys arrive through NavKeyAction (navkeys.h).
+ *
+ * The order comes from the form's own 'fnav' resource when it has one -- that
+ * is what applications that support the navigator ship one per form for -- and
+ * from the objects' top-to-bottom, left-to-right geometry when it does not, so
+ * every dialog is navigable either way.
+ *
+ * Only modal forms take part. Those are the dialogs and menus; a full-screen
+ * form belongs to the application, which may well want the arrow keys itself.
+ *
+ * Selecting is a synthesised tap at the middle of the object, so it goes down
+ * exactly the path the pen does and needs nothing from the application. The
+ * ring is an inverted frame, which makes taking it down the same operation as
+ * putting it up and leaves no saved bits to get out of step with the screen.
